{
  "response": "# Verdict: REVISE\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\nThis custom dropdown implements the visible 80% of the WAI-ARIA APG Listbox pattern \u2014 correct roles, `aria-expanded`, `aria-controls`, arrow key navigation, and `aria-selected`. However, it is missing the 20% that makes the interaction coherent for assistive technology: **focus is never restored to the trigger button on close (Escape or selection), and the listbox never communicates which option is currently active to screen readers via `aria-activedescendant`.** A keyboard user who presses Escape is stranded on `<body>` with no focus. A screen reader user navigating with arrow keys hears no change in the active option. These are not edge cases \u2014 they are the primary interaction loop.\n\n## Pre-commitment Predictions\n\nBefore reading the code, I predicted the following for a custom dropdown/listbox:\n\n| # | Prediction | Found? |\n|---|-----------|--------|\n| 1 | Focus not restored to button on Escape | **YES \u2014 confirmed, no `buttonRef` exists at all** |\n| 2 | Focus not restored to button on selection | **YES \u2014 confirmed, same root cause** |\n| 3 | Missing `aria-activedescendant` on listbox | **YES \u2014 confirmed, absent** |\n| 4 | Options lack `id` attributes (needed for `aria-activedescendant`) | **YES \u2014 confirmed** |\n| 5 | `<label htmlFor>` misused on a `<button>` | **YES \u2014 confirmed, semantically incorrect** |\n| 6 | Missing Home/End key support | **YES \u2014 confirmed, absent** |\n| 7 | Roving tabindex issues on options | **NO \u2014 not applicable; focus stays on listbox container (correct for this pattern)** |\n\nAll predictions were either confirmed or correctly ruled out. No surprises.\n\n---\n\n## Critical Findings (blocks access)\n\n### C1: Focus is never restored to the trigger button on Escape\n\n**Evidence:** `handleKeyDown` (the Escape branch) calls `setIsOpen(false)` and nothing else. There is no `buttonRef` anywhere in the component. The `<ul>` is conditionally rendered (`{isOpen && ...}`), so when `isOpen` becomes `false`, the element is removed from the DOM. Focus, which was on the `<ul>`, is orphaned and falls to `<body>`.\n\n```jsx\n// The Escape handler \u2014 line-by-line:\n} else if (e.key === 'Escape') {\n  e.preventDefault();\n  setIsOpen(false);          // \u2190 list unmounts, focus is lost to <body>\n  // \u2190 NO buttonRef.current.focus() call\n}\n```\n\nThe component stores a `listRef` to focus the list on open, but **never stores a reference to the button** to restore focus on close. The button has `id=\"dropdown-btn\"` but no `ref`.\n\n**User group impacted:** Keyboard-only users (all), screen reader users (all)\n\n**WCAG / APG citation:**\n- **WCAG 2.4.3 Focus Order** \u2014 after the dropdown closes, the next Tab stop should be the button (or the element after the dropdown), not `<body>`.\n- **WAI-ARIA APG Listbox pattern** \u2014 \"When the listbox is closed, focus returns to the button.\" The pattern explicitly requires focus restoration to the trigger.\n\n**Realist check:** A keyboard user opens the dropdown, presses Escape, and is now on `<body>`. Their next Tab press goes to whatever follows the dropdown in the DOM \u2014 they have skipped the button entirely. They must Tab backward to find it. This is a complete loss of spatial context in the interaction. Detection: immediate, on first Escape press. Fix: straightforward (add a ref, one line). **Severity is correctly CRITICAL \u2014 this is the primary interaction loop, not an edge case.**\n\n**Fix:**\n```jsx\nconst buttonRef = useRef(null);\n\n// On the button:\n<button ref={buttonRef} id=\"dropdown-btn\" ...>\n\n// In the Escape handler:\n} else if (e.key === 'Escape') {\n  e.preventDefault();\n  setIsOpen(false);\n  buttonRef.current?.focus();   // \u2190 restore focus before unmount\n}\n```\n\nCall `focus()` **before** `setIsOpen(false)` so the button is focused while the list is still in the DOM. Alternatively, call it after \u2014 the button is never unmounted, so it works either way. But calling it before is safer against React batching edge cases.\n\n---\n\n### C2: Focus is never restored to the trigger button on selection\n\n**Evidence:** `handleSelect` calls `setIsOpen(false)` with no focus restoration:\n\n```jsx\nconst handleSelect = (index) => {\n  setSelectedIndex(index);\n  onSelect(options[index]);\n  setIsOpen(false);   // \u2190 list unmounts, focus lost\n  // \u2190 NO buttonRef.current.focus() call\n};\n```\n\nThis affects **both** selection paths: Enter key (via `handleKeyDown`) and mouse click (via `onClick` on the `<li>`).\n\n**User group impacted:** Keyboard-only users, screen reader users\n\n**WCAG / APG citation:**\n- **WCAG 2.4.3 Focus Order**\n- **WAI-ARIA APG Listbox pattern** \u2014 \"When an option is selected, focus returns to the button.\"\n\n**Realist check:** A keyboard user selects an option with Enter. The dropdown closes. Focus is on `<body>`. The user has to Tab backward to the button to confirm the selection or interact further. For a mouse user, this is invisible (they click the button again). For a keyboard user, it's a broken interaction loop every single time. **CRITICAL \u2014 this is the happy path, not an edge case.**\n\n**Fix:** Same as C1 \u2014 add `buttonRef.current?.focus()` in `handleSelect`:\n```jsx\nconst handleSelect = (index) => {\n  setSelectedIndex(index);\n  onSelect(options[index]);\n  setIsOpen(false);\n  buttonRef.current?.focus();   // \u2190 restore focus\n};\n```\n\n---\n\n### C3: Missing `aria-activedescendant` \u2014 screen reader cannot track the active option\n\n**Evidence:** The `<ul role=\"listbox\">` has no `aria-activedescendant` attribute. The `<li role=\"option\">` elements have no `id` attributes. When a screen reader user presses ArrowDown, `selectedIndex` changes and `aria-selected` updates, but the listbox itself never announces which option is currently active.\n\n```jsx\n// Current:\n<ul ref={listRef} id=\"dropdown-list\" role=\"listbox\" tabIndex=\"0\" ...>\n  {options.map((option, index) => (\n    <li key={index} role=\"option\" aria-selected={index === selectedIndex} ...>\n    // \u2190 no id attribute\n  ))}\n</ul>\n// \u2190 no aria-activedescendant on the <ul>\n```\n\n**User group impacted:** Screen reader users (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver)\n\n**WCAG / APG citation:**\n- **WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value** \u2014 the active state of the listbox must be programmatically determinable.\n- **WAI-ARIA APG Listbox pattern** \u2014 \"The listbox has `aria-activedescendant` set to the `id` of the currently active option. Arrow keys change `aria-activedescendant`.\"\n\n**Why `aria-selected` alone is insufficient:** In the APG pattern, `aria-selected` indicates the **committed** selection (the value that will be submitted), while `aria-activedescendant` indicates the **currently highlighted** option during navigation. This component conflates the two: pressing ArrowDown immediately changes `aria-selected`, which means the \"selection\" changes before the user commits it with Enter. A screen reader user hears \"selected\" change on every arrow key press, which is misleading \u2014 they haven't selected anything yet, they're just navigating.\n\n**Realist check:** A screen reader user opens the dropdown, hears \"listbox\" (with no name \u2014 see M1), presses ArrowDown three times. They hear no change in the listbox's state. They cannot tell which option is highlighted. They must guess or use a different navigation method. This is a complete loss of the primary navigation mechanism for screen reader users. **CRITICAL.**\n\n**Fix:**\n```jsx\n<ul\n  ref={listRef}\n  id=\"dropdown-list\"\n  role=\"listbox\"\n  aria-activedescendant={`dropdown-option-${selectedIndex}`}\n  aria-label={label}          // \u2190 also fixes M1\n  tabIndex=\"0\"\n  onKeyDown={handleKeyDown}\n>\n  {options.map((option, index) => (\n    <li\n      key={index}\n      id={`dropdown-option-${index}`}   // \u2190 unique id for aria-activedescendant\n      role=\"option\"\n      aria-selected={index === selectedIndex}\n      ...\n    >\n```\n\n> **Note on ID uniqueness:** If multiple instances of this component can render on the same page, the hardcoded `dropdown-option-${index}` IDs will collide. Use `useId()` (React 18+) or a `useRef`-generated prefix to ensure uniqueness.\n\n---\n\n## Major Findings (significantly degrades experience)\n\n### M1: Listbox has no accessible name\n\n**Evidence:** The `<ul role=\"listbox\">` has `id=\"dropdown-list\"` but no `aria-label`, `aria-labelledby`, or other naming mechanism. A screen reader user who focuses the listbox hears only \"listbox\" with no context about what it contains.\n\n```jsx\n<ul ref={listRef} id=\"dropdown-list\" role=\"listbox\" tabIndex=\"0\" ...>\n// \u2190 no aria-label, no aria-labelledby\n```\n\n**User group impacted:** Screen reader users\n\n**WCAG / APG citation:**\n- **WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value** \u2014 all widgets must have an accessible name.\n- **WAI-ARIA APG Listbox pattern** \u2014 the listbox should have an accessible name that describes its purpose.\n\n**Fix:** Add `aria-label={label}` to the `<ul>`, or use `aria-labelledby` pointing to the label element's id.\n\n---\n\n### M2: `<label htmlFor>` is semantically incorrect for a `<button>`\n\n**Evidence:**\n```jsx\n<label htmlFor=\"dropdown-btn\">{label}</label>\n<button id=\"dropdown-btn\" ...>\n```\n\nThe `<label>` element's `htmlFor` attribute is designed to associate a label with a **form control** (`<input>`, `<select>`, `<textarea>`, `<button>` is technically allowed by the HTML spec, but the semantic intent is form labeling). The button already has an accessible name from its text content (`{options[selectedIndex]}` + the hidden arrow). The `<label>` is redundant for AT and semantically misleading.\n\n**User group impacted:** Screen reader users (minor \u2014 the button already has a name from its content)\n\n**WCAG / APG citation:**\n- **WCAG 1.3.1 Info and Relationships** \u2014 labels should be associated with the correct element type.\n\n**Why this is MAJOR and not MINOR:** The `<label>` provides a **visible** label for the dropdown, which is important for sighted users. But the mechanism is wrong. If a developer later adds `aria-labelledby` to the button pointing to the label's id, the button's accessible name becomes the label text instead of the selected option \u2014 breaking the \"shows current value\" behavior. The correct pattern is:\n\n```jsx\n<span id=\"dropdown-label\">{label}</span>\n<button\n  aria-labelledby=\"dropdown-label\"\n  aria-haspopup=\"listbox\"\n  aria-expanded={isOpen}\n  aria-controls=\"dropdown-list\"\n  ...\n>\n```\n\nOr simply keep the `<label>` as a visual element without `htmlFor`, and let the button's text content serve as its accessible name.\n\n**Fix:** Replace `<label htmlFor=\"dropdown-btn\">` with `<span id=\"dropdown-label\">`, and add `aria-labelledby=\"dropdown-label\"` to the button if the label text should be part of the button's accessible name.\n\n---\n\n### M3: Conflation of \"active\" and \"selected\" states during navigation\n\n**Evidence:** Arrow key handlers immediately update `selectedIndex`, which drives `aria-selected`:\n\n```jsx\n} else if (e.key === 'ArrowDown') {\n  e.preventDefault();\n  const nextIndex = selectedIndex < options.length - 1 ? selectedIndex + 1 : 0;\n  setSelectedIndex(nextIndex);   // \u2190 changes aria-selected immediately\n}\n```\n\nIn the APG Listbox pattern, arrow keys change the **active** option (tracked via `aria-activedescendant`), not the **selected** option (tracked via `aria-selected`). Selection is committed on Enter/Space. This component treats every arrow key press as a selection change.\n\n**User group impacted:** Screen reader users\n\n**WCAG / APG citation:**\n- **WAI-ARIA APG Listbox pattern** \u2014 distinguishes between the active (highlighted) option and the selected (committed) option.\n\n**Impact:** A screen reader user hears \"selected\" change on every arrow key press. They cannot distinguish between \"I'm looking at option 3\" and \"I've selected option 3.\" This is confusing, especially in a multi-step form where the user is still navigating.\n\n**Fix:** Introduce a separate `activeIndex` state for navigation, and only update `selectedIndex` (and `aria-selected`) on Enter/Space:\n\n```jsx\nconst [activeIndex, setActiveIndex] = useState(0);\nconst [selectedIndex, setSelectedIndex] = useState(0);\n\n// Arrow keys change activeIndex (\u2192 aria-activedescendant)\n// Enter/Space sets selectedIndex = activeIndex (\u2192 aria-selected)\n```\n\n> **Pragmatic note:** For a simple single-select dropdown where the displayed value IS the selection, this conflation is a common simplification and the real-world impact is moderate. If the component is used in a context where \"selection\" has a distinct meaning (e.g., a form field), fix it. If it's a display-only dropdown, the `aria-activedescendant` fix (C3) is the priority.\n\n---\n\n## Minor Findings (friction but workaround exists)\n\n### m1: Missing Home/End key support\n\nThe APG Listbox pattern includes Home (jump to first option) and End (jump to last option) keys. These are absent from `handleKeyDown`. A keyboard user navigating a long list must press ArrowDown N times to reach the last option.\n\n**WCAG / APG citation:** WAI-ARIA APG Listbox pattern \u2014 \"Home: Move focus to the first option. End: Move focus to the last option.\"\n\n**Fix:**\n```jsx\n} else if (e.key === 'Home') {\n  e.preventDefault();\n  setActiveIndex(0);\n} else if (e.key === 'End') {\n  e.preventDefault();\n  setActiveIndex(options.length - 1);\n}\n```\n\n### m2: Hardcoded IDs create collision risk\n\n`id=\"dropdown-btn\"` and `id=\"dropdown-list\"` are hardcoded. If two instances of `BuggyDropdown` render on the same page, the IDs collide, and `aria-controls` / `htmlFor` references break.\n\n**Fix:** Use `useId()` (React 18+) or a `useRef`-generated prefix:\n```jsx\nconst id = useId();\n// id=\"dropdown-btn\" \u2192 id={`${id}-btn`}\n// id=\"dropdown-list\" \u2192 id={`${id}-list`}\n```\n\n### m3: No click-outside handler\n\nIf the user clicks outside the dropdown while it's open, the list remains open and focused. There is no `useEffect` with a `mousedown`/`pointerdown` listener to close the list and restore focus. This is a UX gap that also affects keyboard users who Tab away from the list \u2014 the list stays open.\n\n**Fix:** Add a `useEffect` that listens for `pointerdown` on `document` and closes the list if the click is outside the dropdown wrapper.\n\n---\n\n## Enhancements (best practice not met)\n\n- **Type-ahead:** The APG Listbox pattern includes type-ahead (typing the first letter of an option jumps to it). Absent here. Low priority for a small option list.\n- **`aria-label` on the listbox** (covered in M1) \u2014 also consider `aria-label` on the button that includes the label text, e.g., `aria-label={`${label}: ${options[selectedIndex]}`}`.\n- **Reduced motion:** No animations are present, so `prefers-reduced-motion` is not applicable. No action needed.\n- **Touch target size:** The button and list items should be at least 24\u00d724 CSS pixels (WCAG 2.5.8). Cannot verify from JSX alone \u2014 check the CSS.\n\n---\n\n## What's Missing (gap analysis)\n\n| Gap | Why it matters |\n|-----|---------------|\n| **`buttonRef` for focus restoration** | The component has a `listRef` for focusing the list on open, but no corresponding `buttonRef` for restoring focus on close. The asymmetry is the root cause of C1 and C2. |\n| **`aria-activedescendant`** | Without it, screen reader users have no programmatic way to know which option is highlighted during arrow key navigation. |\n| **`id` attributes on `<li>` options** | Required for `aria-activedescendant` to reference them. |\n| **Accessible name on the listbox** | The listbox is anonymous to screen readers. |\n| **Home/End key handlers** | APG pattern requirement for listbox navigation. |\n| **Click-outside / focus-outside handler** | The list can remain open indefinitely if the user clicks or Tabs away. |\n| **`useEffect` for focus management** | No lifecycle hook coordinates focus between open/close states. All focus logic is in event handlers, which is fragile. |\n\n---\n\n## Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n### Screen reader user (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver)\nOpens the dropdown, hears \"listbox\" with no name (M1). Presses ArrowDown \u2014 hears no change in the listbox's state because `aria-activedescendant` is absent (C3). The `aria-selected` changes, but the user cannot distinguish \"navigating\" from \"selecting\" (M3). Presses Escape \u2014 focus is lost to `<body>`, the user is stranded (C1). **This component is effectively unusable for screen reader navigation.**\n\n### Keyboard-only user\nOpens the dropdown with Enter/Space on the button. List receives focus (correct). Arrow keys work (correct). Presses Escape \u2014 focus is lost to `<body>` (C1). Must Tab backward to find the button. Selects an option with Enter \u2014 focus is lost to `<body>` (C2). Must Tab backward. **The interaction loop is broken on every close action.**\n\n### Low vision user (200% zoom, high contrast)\nThe `<label>` provides a visible label (good). The `\u25bc` arrow is `aria-hidden` (good \u2014 no redundant announcement). Focus visibility depends on CSS (cannot verify from JSX). The conditional render (`{isOpen && ...}`) means the list appears/disappears in the DOM, which is fine for reflow. **No specific low-vision blockers identified in the code.**\n\n### Cognitive accessibility\nThe conflation of \"active\" and \"selected\" (M3) could confuse a user who is still deciding which option to pick. The error-free interaction (no validation, no error states) is appropriate for a simple dropdown. **Low cognitive load overall, but the state conflation adds unnecessary mental overhead.**\n\n### Perspective Alarm Levels\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | **HIGH** | Missing `aria-activedescendant`, no listbox name, state conflation |\n| Keyboard-only | **HIGH** | Focus restoration missing on both close paths |\n| Low vision | LOW | No specific code-level issues; CSS-dependent |\n| Cognitive | MEDIUM | Active/selected conflation adds mental overhead |\n| Vestibular & motion | LOW | No animations or auto-playing content |\n| Auditory access | LOW | No media elements |\n| Environmental contrast | LOW | No contrast issues in code; CSS-dependent |\n\n---\n\n## Verdict Justification\n\n**REVISE** \u2014 three CRITICAL findings block core interaction for two user categories (keyboard and screen reader). The component implements the visible ARIA attributes correctly, which is why it would pass automated checks (axe-core would see `aria-expanded`, `aria-haspopup`, `role=\"listbox\"`, `aria-selected`, `aria-controls` \u2014 all present and valid). But the interaction model is incomplete:\n\n1. **Focus restoration is absent** (C1, C2) \u2014 the component has a `listRef` for focusing on open but no `buttonRef` for restoring on close. This is not a subtle edge case; it's the primary close path.\n2. **Active state is not communicated** (C3) \u2014 `aria-activedescendant` is missing, so screen reader users cannot track navigation.\n3. **The listbox is anonymous** (M1) \u2014 no accessible name.\n\nThese are not violations that axe-core would flag (the ARIA attributes that *are* present are valid). They are **design gaps** \u2014 the pattern is 80% implemented, and the missing 20% is exactly what assistive technology needs.\n\n**To upgrade to ACCEPT-WITH-RESERVATIONS:** Fix C1, C2, C3, and M1. The M2 and M3 findings can be addressed in a follow-up.\n\n**To upgrade to ACCEPT:** Fix all CRITICAL and MAJOR findings, add Home/End keys, and verify with a screen reader (NVDA or VoiceOver) that arrow key navigation announces the active option and that Escape/selection restore focus to the button.\n\n---\n\n## Open Questions (unscored)\n\n1. **Is this component used in a context where \"selection\" has a distinct meaning from \"displayed value\"?** If yes, the M3 conflation is a real problem. If it's a display-only dropdown, the `aria-activedescendant` fix (C3) is sufficient and M3 can be deferred.\n\n2. **Can multiple instances of this component render on the same page?** If yes, the hardcoded IDs (m2) are a real collision risk, not a theoretical one.\n\n3. **Is there a CSS `:focus` style on the listbox and options?** The code doesn't show focus styling. If the listbox has no visible focus indicator, that's a WCAG 2.4.7 (Focus Visible) issue that can't be assessed from JSX alone.\n\n4. **What is the `onSelect` callback expected to do?** If it triggers a form submission or page navigation, the focus restoration timing becomes more critical (the component may unmount before focus can be restored).\n\n5. **Is there a parent component that manages focus?** If the parent has a focus trap or focus management system, the missing `buttonRef` might be compensated for externally. Unlikely, but worth confirming.",
